cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this paper or its Lean 4 verification artifact, please cite it as below."
title: "Frozen Memory and Open Inversion: A Conditional Risk-Gap Bound"
abstract: >-
  This paper asks whether a single fixed-memory component can be complete on its own for open
  inversion tasks: inverse problems whose observations are ill-posed, whose required context is
  heterogeneous and addressed by the evidence, and whose inference inputs are not constrained by the
  training distribution. It separates values instantiated from evidence and analytic invariants
  (Mode A) from values frozen at inference from corpus statistics or manual setting (Mode B). For
  mask-modeled inputs, under a fixed test distribution, integrable squared loss, a fixed
  evidence-instantiated benchmark, a specified offline-frozen class with a uniform bounded-description
  rate, and barrier and scale-restricted realization conditions, it proves a conditional risk gap
  kappa = p delta^2 / 16 - risk*. When kappa > 0, no rule in that class achieves uniform tolerance
  0 < epsilon < kappa. A minimal instance shows the hypotheses are jointly satisfiable. The neural
  corollary additionally assumes output variation on an evidence-collision pair. The result limits
  solo use; it does not deny the usefulness of frozen components in composite systems, and it does not
  establish BD, P4, or real-world applicability. The algebraic core and a toy closure are formalized
  in Lean 4 with Mathlib, without sorry. This repository holds the JMLR LaTeX source, the compiled
  paper, and the Lean 4 artifact.
